---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const treeCode = `const top = chart({
  mark: "bar",
  data: totals,
  encoding: { x: "id", y: "value" },
});

const matrix = chart({
  mark: "matrix",
  data: cells,
  encoding: { x: "id", group: "set", y: "active" },
});

const body = stackX([leftBars, matrix, detailChart]);
const view = stackY([top, body]);`;

const embedCode = `const events = repeat(eventRows, (event) =>
  chart({
    mark: "pie",
    data: event.parts,
    encoding: { x: "category", y: "value" },
  }),
);

const sequence = embed(
  sequenceContainer({ xDomain: weeks, yDomain: learners }),
  events,
  { x: "week", y: "learner", key: "id" },
);`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Core Concepts | composite.js">
  <div class="docs-content">
    <header class="docs-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Concepts</p>
      <h1>Charts and their relationships</h1>
      <p>
        A composite view starts with individual charts and the relationships
        among them. You describe those relationships; composite.js turns them
        into a measured and positioned layout tree.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>The user-facing model</h2>
      <p>
        Users make two kinds of decisions: what each chart shows, and how those
        charts are related. A relationship can place views in a horizontal or
        vertical sequence, repeat a view across a domain, align related views,
        or embed child views in a semantic container.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are abstract layout decisions. They do not require SVG transforms,
        pixel coordinates, or manual bounding-box calculations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Chart leaves</h2>
      <p>
        A chart leaf owns a mark, its data rows, and an encoding object. Encoding
        values are field names from your rows, while keys such as <code>x</code>,
        <code>y</code>, <code>group</code>, and <code>size</code> describe
        visual channels.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Composition relationships</h2>
      <p>
        <code>stackX()</code> expresses a left-to-right relationship and
        <code>stackY()</code> expresses a top-to-bottom relationship. The
        layout engine derives concrete positions from the measured children.
        Because compositions can be nested, the same operators build both
        simple pairs and larger analytical views.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{treeCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Repeats and embedding</h2>
      <p>
        <code>repeatX()</code> and <code>repeatY()</code> create small multiples
        along one axis. Use directionless <code>repeat()</code> with
        <code>embed()</code> when repeated children should be placed into slots
        from a container such as <code>sequenceContainer()</code>.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{embedCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>The layout-node mechanism</h2>
      <p>
        Internally, charts and compositions share one layout-node contract.
        Chart leaves come from <code>chart()</code>, annotations come from
        <code>text()</code>, decorated subtrees come from <code>wrapper()</code>, and
        composition nodes come from stack, repeat, and embed helpers. This
        shared contract is what makes recursive composition possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Composition helpers expect layout nodes, so wrap each chart
        configuration with <code>chart(&#123;...&#125;)</code> before composing it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Automatic layout and optional refinement</h2>
      <p>
        When options are omitted, composite.js supplies dimensions, margins,
        repeat padding, and default stack alignment, then measures and positions
        the complete tree. Explicit options refine that result. For example,
        <code>margin</code> changes a stack gap, while <code>align</code> can make
        a top chart align with a matrix nested inside the row below it. These
        controls are available when the intended relationship cannot be inferred
        from the basic composition alone.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
